Date: Thu, 9 Dec 93 10:54:56 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Brave New Extropia? No Thanks.

David Friedman and Arthur D. Hlavaty exchange quotes on male femininity...

> [...]

   In that context, it is interesting to note that essentially all humans
operate with the same complement of *functioning* chromosomes, which being
one "X" and two each of twenty-two non-sex chromosomes:  The "Y" chromosome
may contain only one active gene, which acts only briefly, in early embryo
growth, to cause the development of testes rather than ovaries, and human
cells in general have the ability that, if they are provided with more
than one "X" chromosome, they will shut down all but one of them.

(Reference and caveats:  Upper-division classes in genetics and in human
heredity and evolution, San Jose State University, 1992.  "Y" containing
only one gene is not certain, but Y-linked traits would be fairly obvious
in studies of human genetics and there seem to be none known for certain,
beyond maleness.  The shut-down "X"s have some residual effect; thus
XXY individuals are phenotypically distinguishable from XY individuals.)


(The details of when and how the "X"s get shut down are interesting -- it
happens fairly early on in embryo development, each cell appears to select
randomly which "X" shall survive, and propagates that decision to its
descendants as growth continues.  Thus XX individuals end up literally
a patchwork quilt of two phenotypically different kinds of tissue -- one
in which the X inherited from the male parent is expressed, one in which
the X inherited from the female parent is expressed.

Human females do not look like patchwork quilts because there seem to be no
human X-linked genes which have multiple alelles (spelling?) that give rise
to noticeable differences in external appearance, but in cats -- I think
the shut-down-X mechanism exists in all mammals -- there is an X-linked
gene that has to do with whether non-white fur is black or yellow, so that
when you look at a tortoise-shell or calico cat you are in fact seeing
the patchwork, and you can be confident that the animal has two (or more)
X chromosomes.  XX's will be garden-variety females, XXY's will be rare,
and will be equipped as males but likely sterile -- another side effect
of XXY sex chromosomes.  Thus the folklore that male calico cats are
rare and sterile is in essence correct.

My, how I blather on...)

