Some comments on this entire thread, as I have seen it unfold:  With  
regard to the debate style of truth-seeking, I see some merit to it.   
Since "losing" hurts, one is highly _motivated_ to examine one's  
"opponent's" arguments very carefully, and to find rebuttals, if at  
all possible.  Those for whom losing hurts a great deal (a good  
survival trait, I imagine), this motivation can bring out the best,  
and consequently is perhaps the best feature of the debate style of  
truth-seeking. (I have heard that it is loss-avoidance (as opposed to  
win-attraction) that most _motivates_ [most] champion competitive  
athletes.)

This is not to say that people who like to play with ideas cannot be  
highly motivated to fully explore an idea's consequences.  An idea is  
like a toy.  With playful curiosity, we avidly explore all its  
features and uses.  (I am at my best with regard to mathematical  
creativity when I am in this state of mind, rather than debate mode.)

Taken to a perhaps simplistic level of abstraction, we may  
characterize these 2 styles of truth-seeking as war vs. play, and the  
state of mind as idea warrior vs. idea child.

While I believe that both of these personalities are in all of us, I  
also believe that a playful environment is happier than a warlike  
environment.  (The warlike environment may be more thrilling.)  This  
list, I think, would profit from being as happy a place to "be" as we  
can make it.  (And winning is not the only way to be thrilled.)

Tim May has said that he enters the "idea child" mental state (my  
words, and maybe a bad characterization), when he sufficiently  
respects, and feels sufficiently respected by, the person with whom  
he is working.  Aren't we, on this list, better served by lowering  
the threshold leading to that state of mind?  Given the rarity in the  
general population of the values held in common on this list, it is  
reasonable, I believe, to _presume_ that everyone who has joined this  
list is sufficiently intelligent and well-meaning (unless they give  
evidence to the contrary, and even then, it is likely only a  
transitory mood) for us to accept them as "idea playmates."

Pete

