depth of knowledge certainly exists on Extropians and Cypherpunks, the
other list where I'm a bit player.

The main difference between a formal debate and a net.discussion is that
there's no need to declare a winner after an artificial time constraint on
the net (each debate lasts around one hour and forty-five minutes).  In
fact, I prefer to think that the open-ended nature of the list discussions
implies that consensus may eventually be reached on individual issues, but
also that underlying personal philosophies are modified slowly with time. 
While emotion certainly enters into the picture on the net, there isn't the
same sense of urgency one gets when it's your side's last speech and there
are certain critical issues you're getting killed on.

I judge policy debates for a league called the NDT (National Debate
Tournament) that emphasizes evidence, and I personally enforce a strong
distinction between logic and assertion.  When someone says "that evidence
(quote from a published source) makes a clearly counter-intuitive claim and
you should ignore it" I hear "man, we can't afford to lose the issue but we
have nothing to back up our claims".  Yes, you get a lot of truly
outlandish arguments that just happen to have been made in the literature
(anything from _Marxist Review_ to _The New Republic_, with all stops in
between and many on the far fringes), but it prevents the debaters from
out-and-out lying to save their own skins, leaving the judge (one per round
during the preliminary debates) with something concrete to base a decision
on, instead of sighing and deciding which side spouted the better rhetoric.

The point:  in a discussion I'm solid rhetorically, but I always give
credence to expert opinion.  Besides, most of the people who post regularly
on Extropians and Cypherpunks have been arguing these and other issues for
years, whether in coffee shops or conferences.  Mix in technical knowledge
and you have a solid combination.

>Since you've seen a lot of formal debate, and you think we do it well
>given the odd nature of the medium, I'd like to hear what you think of
>the role of debate vs. other modes of discussion.
>
>There was a proposal a while back to isolate debates on the list by
>marking them DEBATE: and having a formal structure. Then anyone who
>was feeling argumentative could announce a DEBATE: on his chosen topic
>and those who wanted a more mutual style of discussion could go sit by
>the fire and figure things out without keeping score. As usual, this
>idea died for want of anyone to carry it on, the population of the
>list not really quite high enough for this or the multiple-rooms idea
>to fly yet.

I don't think adding formality to list discussions would be beneficial in
most cases, though I wouldn't discourage others who disagree.  Here's my
reasoning:


1)  Topics covered:

  Academic debate centers around an annual topic with many sub-parts
(Commander in Chief powers include troop deployment, grand strategy,
funding, environmental damage etc.)

  List discussions center around various themes tied together by a common
element (Extropian values, in this case)


2)  Argumentational style

  Academic debate, due to the inherent time constraints, encourages brevity
and high-content argumentation with constant appeals to authority.  Flowery
rhetoric is fine when you're summing things up at the end of a speech (15
seconds or so, the rest is information), but more than that means you're
giving your opponents a time advantage (less stuff to cover in an
equivalent time).

  On the list, most of us have jobs/classes/families/social
lives/pets/hobbies/addictions and whatever else, so we don't have all the
time in the world to devote to these discussions, either.  The rules of
evidence are relaxed somewhat due to common interest and the nature of the
discussion, which is generally less antagonistic than is required for a
zero-sum proposition.  This consideration is offset by the generally higher
level of personal knowledge about and experience with the topics discussed
on the list.

3)  Social organization

  The debate community is like a huge fraternity, with thousands of members
from across the US.  I went to Syracuse and had good friends from Miami
(Florida), Florida State, UCLA, USC, Cal State Long Beach, University of
Chicago, the US Military Academy (West Point), William and Mary, Western
Washington University, Emerson College (Boston), and numerous other places.
 When you see a common group of folks one or two weekends a month for four
years (assuming you enter college at the same time and don't quit the
activity), you form strong alliances and antagonisms.

  The list is very much the same, though global.  We "see" each other
almost every day and come into voice or personal contact on occasion,
building those bonds of sympathy and antipathy while furthering (usually)
our knowledge base and honing our argumentation skills.


Adding structure to certain discussions might be entertaining, but think
what would occur:  the issues probably would have been discussed here
before, so much time would be spent going over well-known ground.  If the
observors aren't list denizens, think of the time required to bring them up
to speed.

Now, think of these points in relation to the atrocities called "debates"
that come up on national television every now and again and tell me we
should follow that model :-).  Not meant to be a flame to the folks who
would like more structure, but you need to be careful in establishing a
framework that retains the information base and encourages real information
exchange and argumentation.  What you get in normal television "debates" is
a series of monlogues and evasions.  Also, imagine the abuse that a
media-savvy candidate can inflict on us.  How many of you were swayed by
Reagan's answer to the age question when he was facing Mondale?  If you can
think of it without my help ("I won't make an issue of my opponent's youth
and inexperience"), you should realize the danger of demagogues.  I know I
was affected.

When it's a free-for all, it's much easier to jump in and scream "bullshit"
en masse.  If the poster has some real points to make, he'll need to make
them then (if he didn't up front) or face the consequences of losing the
argument.

Discussion?  Other former debaters on the list with opinions?

